                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6c1ae4f0-0e24-47ba-aa09-0e58788b1e89",
                        "source_entry_id": "90f70623-76f5-33b6-a4a0-d0b14554ca26",
                        "source_sense_id": "ed3c8fe1-68c4-32bc-942a-e8e78495c12a",
                        "target_lang_word": "threne",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "threne",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "49303605-1a4b-352b-9e7e-3495d110ff83",
                                        "entry_id": "cff30cc1-c374-30c7-9317-fd96dfaf965e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "a dirge or lamentation"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7dd0bd7c-7409-4885-aed1-490a7493e79e",
                                                "sense_id": "49303605-1a4b-352b-9e7e-3495d110ff83",
                                                "text": "1874, James Thomson, The City of Dreadful Night, XXI\nThat City's sombre Patroness and Queen,\nIn bronze sublimity she gazes forth\nOver her Capital of teen and threne"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8ce448f5-0b43-4996-b28f-593356cb1ec2",
                                                "sense_id": "49303605-1a4b-352b-9e7e-3495d110ff83",
                                                "text": "A truce to threnes and trentals and jeremies and all such congenital defunctive music."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "96d8cc8f-def0-4ef2-a811-3836b23babeb",
                        "source_entry_id": "90f70623-76f5-33b6-a4a0-d0b14554ca26",
                        "source_sense_id": "ed3c8fe1-68c4-32bc-942a-e8e78495c12a",
                        "target_lang_word": "threnody",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "threnody",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3237b5c8-311d-354b-9528-580a6c5ed248",
                                        "entry_id": "52565089-c401-3514-b48b-38914a7930e5",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A song or poem of lamentation or mourning for a dead person; a dirge; an elegy."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "89a41e25-f733-4cf7-941c-40fcd2dd04d5",
                                                "sense_id": "3237b5c8-311d-354b-9528-580a6c5ed248",
                                                "text": "The fifer actually knew but one tune \"The Bonnie Blue Flag\"—and did not know that well. But it was all that he had, and he played it with wearisome monotony for every camp call. . . . I never hated any piece of music as I came to hate that threnody of treason."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cfb914de-d822-4e78-8c45-2e9eb5222479",
                                                "sense_id": "3237b5c8-311d-354b-9528-580a6c5ed248",
                                                "text": "A strongly personal note runs through Kenneth Leighton's Improvisation (Novello, 35p), which is a threnody in memory of Maurice de Sausmārez. Gently undulating, cantabile lines of ‘mourning’ alternate with sharp, anguished note-clusters which work up to a fierce paroxysm of grief."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
